<title>USA: US oil analyst sees uneven initial Iraqi oil flows.</title>
<headline>US oil analyst sees uneven initial Iraqi oil flows.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1996-11-12</dateline>
<text>
<p>Even after Iraq is allowed to sell oil under the United Nations oil-for-food scheme, initial oil flows will likely be uneven and unpredictable, according to Frederick Leuffer, senior managing director of Bear, Stearns and Co.</p>
<p>Speaking at the American Petroleum Institute's annual meeting, Leuffer said he foresaw Iraqi oil returning to market in the second half of 1997, but thought Iraqi transgressions would ultimately disrupt the plan.</p>
<p>Leuffer said he thought it was only a matter of time before the plan would be disrupted by aid being diverted to the army or by some type of military flareup in the northern Kurdish regions or the country's southeast region.</p>
<p>He said oil markets continue to fear Iraqi oil returning, but noted that once the uncertainty ends, he expects the market will adapt after an initial price decline.</p>
</text>
